 🔹 html, body { ... }

This is a group selector.

👉 It means: apply the same styles to both the <html> and <body> elements.

<html> → root of the page
<body> → visible content area

💡 Why both?
Because sometimes height-related styles don’t work properly unless both are set.
html,body{ height: 100%;}

🔹 height: 100%;

👉 This sets the height of both <html> and <body> to 100% of the viewport (screen height).

Without this, body height depends on content
With this, body takes full screen height

💡 Useful for:

Full-screen layouts
Flexbox or centering content vertically
| Feature      | `html`            | `body`                |
| ------------ | ----------------- | --------------------- |
| Position     | Root element      | Inside `html`         |
| Purpose      | Structure of page | Visible content       |
| Contains     | `head` + `body`   | UI elements           |
| CSS Usage    | Global settings   | Layout & design       |
| Units impact | Controls `rem`    | Doesn’t control `rem` |

-->🔹 Short Answer

👉 Yes, if the parent height decreases:

height: 100% → child will reduce
min-height: 100% → child will also reduce (minimum is based on parent)

BUT the real difference shows when content is involved.

🔍 Case 1: No extra content inside

Parent = 500px → then reduced to 300px

With height: 100%
.child {
  height: 100%;
}

👉 Child = exactly same as parent

500px → becomes 300px ✔
With min-height: 100%
.child {
  min-height: 100%;
}

👉 Child = at least parent height

500px → becomes 300px ✔

✅ So in this case: both behave same

🔥 Case 2: Content inside child (IMPORTANT)

Parent = 300px
Content inside child needs = 600px

❌ With height: 100%

👉 Child is forced to stay 300px

Content overflows ❌
Layout breaks
✅ With min-height: 100%

👉 Child starts at 300px but can grow

Expands to 600px ✔
No overflow
🧠 Key Understanding
height: 100% → strict / fixed
min-height: 100% → flexible / expandable
🔁 So your exact question:

if we reduce parent height then both height reduces?

👉 ✅ YES, both will reduce
👉 ❗ BUT:

height → always forced to match parent
min-height → only minimum, can grow if needed
🧱 Simple Analogy
height = "You must be exactly this tall"
min-height = "You must be at least this tall, but can grow"
🚀 Practical Tip (important)

For sections like .pimg1:

👉 Best:

min-height: 100vh;


