🔹 html { font-size: 62.5%; }

This is a very common technique used in CSS for easier sizing with rem.

✅ What it means
By default, most browsers set:
1rem = 16px
When you write:
html {
    font-size: 62.5%;
}

It changes root font size to:

62.5% of 16px = 10px
✅ Why developers use this

Because 10px is easier to calculate than 16px.

So now:

1rem = 10px
✅ Easy conversion
rem	px
1rem	10px
1.6rem	16px
2rem	20px
3rem	30px

👉 Just multiply by 10

✅ Benefits
Easier math (no need to divide by 16)
Cleaner and more readable code
Helps in responsive design
Works well with rem units instead of px
✅ Example
html {
    font-size: 62.5%;
}

body {
    font-size: 1.6rem; /* 16px */
}

h1 {
    font-size: 3.2rem; /* 32px */
}
⚠️ Important Notes
This affects the entire website because html is the root.
Some users change browser default font size → this still respects accessibility.
Avoid mixing too much px with rem.

